        <p class="section-note">
          <strong>Decided 2026-08-02 (JES-155, choice 2): option C, <code>var(--deep-space)</code>
          fill + <code>var(--light-pink)</code> text.</strong> Replaces the three grays
          (<code>#6c757d</code> Bootstrap, <code>#607d8b</code> Material, and the <code>#5a6268</code>
          hover-darken that rode along with them) that all meant "secondary button". On-brand: no new
          color enters the palette. Applies to <code>.end-game-actions</code>,
          <code>.card-action-button.secondary</code>, and <code>.modal-action-button.secondary</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="options">
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">.end-game-actions button</div>
            <div class="stage stage-dark">
              <button class="pushable-flat" style="background-color: var(--deep-space); color: var(--light-pink); box-shadow: 0 4px 0 #0d0716, 0 7px 7px hsl(0deg 0% 0% / 0.25);">Restart game</button>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">game.css</div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">.modal-action-button.secondary</div>
            <div class="stage stage-dark">
              <button class="modal-action-button secondary">Cancel</button>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">playmat.css — card modal</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="choice">
          <div class="choice-question">
            The seven rainbow card-modal action buttons: keep color-coded actions, or collapse to a
            primary/secondary pair?
          </div>
          <div class="options">
            <div class="option">
              <div class="option-label">A — Keep color coding (current)</div>
              <div class="stage stage-dark">
                <button class="modal-action-button play-button">Play</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button put-in-hand-button">Put in Hand</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button put-on-top-button">Put on Top</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button put-on-bottom-button">Put on Bottom</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button flip-button">Flip</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button gatherer-button">Gatherer</button>
              </div>
              <div class="option-where">.modal-action-button.* in playmat.css</div>
              <div class="option-note">
                Each destination has its own hue, so you learn the colors. But the hues are seven
                unrelated Material values and none of them are brand colors.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <div class="option-label">B — One primary, rest secondary</div>
              <div class="stage stage-dark">
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #bb5277; color: white;">Play</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #221534; color: var(--light-pink);">Put in Hand</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #221534; color: var(--light-pink);">Put on Top</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #221534; color: var(--light-pink);">Put on Bottom</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #221534; color: var(--light-pink);">Flip</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #221534; color: var(--light-pink);">Gatherer</button>
              </div>
              <div class="option-where">var(--dark-pink) / var(--deep-space)</div>
              <div class="option-note">
                Two tokens instead of seven hexes, and the most likely action stands out. Loses the
                learnable per-destination color.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ================================================================ -->
      <section class="gallery-section" id="type">
        <h2>Typography <span class="badge badge-standard">standard</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
          Three faces, used with real discipline. This is the most consistent thing in the app —
          don't introduce a fourth.
        </p>

        <div class="type-row">
          <div class="type-meta">Risque 400 · display<br>hero title, slogan, step verbs</div>
          <span style="font-family: 'Risque', cursive; font-size: 3rem; color: white;">Shuffle Up</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-row">
          <div class="type-meta">Orbitron 700 · headings + chrome<br>nav, buttons, titles, tiles, inputs</div>
          <span style="font-family: 'Orbitron', sans-serif; font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.75rem; color: white;">MTG Deck Shuffler</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-row">
          <div class="type-meta">Orbitron 400 · nav links</div>
          <span style="font-family: 'Orbitron', sans-serif; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1.5rem; color: white;">play · docs · about</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-row">
          <div class="type-meta">Ovo 400 · body + card names<br>prose, card names, metadata</div>
          <span style="font-family: 'Ovo', serif; font-size: 1.1rem; color: white;">Atraxa, Praetors' Voice</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-row">
          <div class="type-meta">monospace · debug only<br>tab ids, JSON dumps</div>
          <span style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.85rem; color: #9a91a6;">game.browser_tab_id = 3f9c…</span>
        </div>

        <h3>The rule</h3>
        <ul class="rule-list">
          <li><strong>Orbitron</strong> for anything the user acts on or navigates by — buttons, nav, headings, form labels, form fields.</li>
          <li><strong>Ovo</strong> for anything the user reads — prose, and card names specifically. A card name is content, not chrome.</li>
          <li><strong>Risque</strong> only for the big splashy marketing words on the site pages. Never on the play pages.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ================================================================ -->
      <section class="gallery-section" id="geometry">
        <h2>Geometry &amp; spacing <span class="badge badge-choose">choose one</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
          The stated rule is <em>square corners except on physical round elements</em> — cards,
          playmats, the card-back stack. Recent code follows it. Older CSS uses
          <strong>13 different <code>border-radius</code> values</strong>: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 20,
          30, 80px, 50%, 3vh, and 0.
        </p>

        <table class="gallery-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Radius</th><th>Where</th><th>Physical object?</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>join-table panel, hamburger, precon tiles, inputs, <code>.table-cards-button</code>, <code>.go-to-table-button</code></td><td>no — correct</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>2px</code></td><td><code>.cool-command-zone-surround</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>3px</code></td><td><code>.card-action-button</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>4px</code></td><td>menu panel + menu buttons, <code>.modal-close</code>, debug blocks</td><td>no</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>5px</code></td><td><code>.flip-button</code>, <code>.hand-drop-zone</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>6px</code></td><td><code>.card-modal-position-indicator</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>8px</code></td><td><code>.modal-action-button</code>, <code>.card-modal-nav-button</code>, library card-back inner rule</td><td>the inner rule, yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>10px</code></td><td><code>.mtg-card-image</code>, <code>.modal-dialog</code>, <code>.commander-placeholder</code></td><td>cards yes, modal no</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>20px</code></td><td><code>.playmat-prepare</code>, <code>.hand-drop-zone.drag-over</code></td><td>playmat yes — see the 80px row</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>30px</code> / <code>3vh</code></td><td><code>.modal-card-image</code>, <code>.card-modal-image</code></td><td>yes — a big card</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>50%</code></td><td><code>.hand-count</code>, <code>.card-modal-close</code></td><td>arguably — they're tokens/discs</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>80px</code></td><td><code>.playmat-game</code></td><td>yes — but <strong>it's the same object as the 20px row</strong>, one playmat dressed two ways. Open question, not a settled distinction</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="choice">
          <div class="choice-question">
            What does "square corners" mean for chrome that currently has a small radius
            (modals, menu buttons, action buttons)?
          </div>
          <div class="options">
            <div class="option">
              <div class="option-label">A — Truly 0</div>
              <div class="stage stage-dark">
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #bb5277; border-radius: 0;">Play</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #221534; color: var(--light-pink); border-radius: 0;">Cancel</button>
              </div>
              <div class="option-where">border-radius: 0</div>
              <div class="option-note">
                Matches the stated rule exactly, and matches the newest code (join-table, hamburger).
                Hardest-edged; pairs well with the outset bevels.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <div class="option-label">B — A single small radius</div>
              <div class="stage stage-dark">
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #bb5277; border-radius: 4px;">Play</button>
                <button class="modal-action-button" style="background-color: #221534; color: var(--light-pink); border-radius: 4px;">Cancel</button>
              </div>
              <div class="option-where">border-radius: 4px — the de facto value in the game menu</div>
              <div class="option-note">
                Softer, and closer to where most of the CSS already sits, so less to change. But it
                contradicts the written rule.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>Spacing <span class="badge badge-drift">drift</span></h3>
        <p class="section-note">
          There is no scale. Padding, gap, and margin use 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20,
          24, 30 and 40px, intermixed with rem values. A proposed 4px-based scale:
          <code>4 · 8 · 12 · 16 · 24 · 32 · 48</code> — every existing value rounds to one of these
          within 2px except 5, 15, and 18.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ================================================================ -->
      <section class="gallery-section" id="buttons">
        <h2>Buttons <span class="badge badge-standard">standard</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
          <strong>Decided 2026-08-02 (JES-155, choice 1): option C, <code>.pushable-flat</code>.</strong>
          One travel distance, one spring easing, one press-down snap, built from a hard
          <code>box-shadow</code> bevel instead of a browser-drawn <code>outset</code>/<code>inset</code>
          border — so it drops onto an existing <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> with no markup change, and
          the edge color is exact instead of engine-dependent. The base rule lives in
          <code>styles.css</code> (global, since every page loads it); each button site keeps its own
          fill color — colors are separate open choices (2 and 3) — and reproduces this same shape with
          its own darker shadow color.
        </p>

        <div class="options">
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">Big Fat CTA — .button-base .pushable-flat .begin-button</div>
            <div class="stage stage-site">
              <a href="#buttons" class="button-base pushable-flat begin-button">BEGIN</a>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">site.css — white fill, signature chunky light-pink border; not just a scaled-up primary button</div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">Playmat button — .library-buttons button</div>
            <div class="stage stage-playmat">
              <div class="library-buttons" style="grid-template-areas: 'draw draw' 'shuffle shuffle' 'search reveal';">
                <button class="draw-button">Draw</button>
                <button class="shuffle-button">Shuffle</button>
                <button class="search-button">Search</button>
                <button class="reveal-button">Reveal</button>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">playmat.css — same shape, black fill</div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">Modal action — .modal-action-button</div>
            <div class="stage stage-dark">
              <button class="modal-action-button play-button">Play</button>
              <button class="modal-action-button secondary">Cancel</button>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">playmat.css — same shape, per-destination hue (choice 3, still open)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">Menu item — .menu-section button</div>
            <div class="stage stage-dark">
              <div class="game-menu-panel" style="position: static; opacity: 1; transform: none; pointer-events: auto; min-width: 0; width: 100%;">
                <div class="menu-section history-actions">
                  <button>Show history</button>
                </div>
                <div class="menu-section end-game-actions">
                  <button>Restart game</button>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">game.css — same shape, <code>var(--deep-space)</code> fill (choice 2, decided)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">Table section — .table-section</div>
            <div class="stage stage-playmat">
              <div class="table-section" style="width: 220px;">
                <a href="#buttons" class="pushable-flat go-to-table-button">Go to Table: verify-table</a>
                <button class="pushable-flat pushable-dark pushable-small table-cards-button">3 Cards on table</button>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">game.css — primary CTA to the joined table, secondary (choice 2 fill) opens the card list. Solo games (no table joined) show only the full-size "N Cards on table" button.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">Hamburger — .menu-toggle</div>
            <div class="stage stage-playmat">
              <button class="menu-toggle">☰</button>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">game.css — same shape, playmat-one fill</div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">Disabled</div>
            <div class="stage stage-playmat">
              <button class="pushable-flat pushable-small" disabled>Draw</button>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">styles.css — sits flat on the ground, no travel left</div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">Tab / radio pair — .hero-button (decided 2026-08-02, standalone)</div>
            <div class="stage stage-site">
              <div class="hero-buttons">
                <button class="button-base pushable-flat hero-button active">Precon</button>
                <button class="button-base pushable-flat hero-button">Archidekt</button>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">site.css — same fill for both (this is a radio choice, not two independent buttons); the active one gets a dark-pink underline, since elevation alone read as too subtle to signal "this one, not the other"</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>The rule</h3>
        <ul class="rule-list">
          <li>Every button: <code>transform: translateY(-4px)</code> at rest, <code>-6px</code> on
            hover (spring easing, <code>cubic-bezier(.3,.7,.4,1.5)</code>, 250ms), <code>-2px</code> on
            press (34ms snap) — plus a matching two-layer <code>box-shadow</code> (a solid "edge" layer
            and a soft ground shadow) that shrinks and grows with the travel.</li>
          <li><strong>Fill color is not part of this rule</strong> — each site keeps its own background
            and picks a shadow color roughly 60% as light. <code>.pushable-flat</code> in
            <code>styles.css</code> is the reusable drop-in for new buttons; existing components
            reproduce the same numbers directly in their own stylesheet rather than taking on the
            class.</li>
          <li>No more <code>outset</code>/<code>inset</code> borders for press feedback anywhere.</li>
          <li><strong>A radio/tab pair needs a selection signal beyond press physics.</strong>
            <code>.hero-button.active</code> gets a <code>4px</code> dark-pink underline
            (<code>::after</code>) on top of the shared shape — a standalone decision, not part of
            JES-155, made when the underline-only elevation difference proved too subtle.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ================================================================ -->
      <section class="gallery-section" id="focus">
        <h2>Focus states <span class="badge badge-standard">standard</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
          One ring for the whole app: <code>3px solid var(--light-pink)</code> at
          <code>outline-offset: 3px</code>, declared once in <code>styles.css</code> as a
          <code>:focus-visible</code> rule covering <code>a</code>, <code>button</code>,
          <code>input</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>textarea</code>, <code>summary</code>
          and <code>[tabindex]</code>. <code>:focus-visible</code> rather than
          <code>:focus</code>, so it appears for keyboard users and not on mouse clicks —
          which is what the single plain <code>:focus</code> rule it replaced got wrong.
          Decided 2026-08-06.
        </p>
        <p class="section-note">
          <strong>Tab into both stages below.</strong> The gap the offset opens shows the
          <em>page</em> behind the control, not the control's own fill — which is what keeps the
          ring legible even against the CTA's own light-pink border.
        </p>

        <div class="options">
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">On the playmat — the strong ground</div>
            <div class="stage stage-playmat">
              <button class="pushable-flat pushable-small">Tab to me</button>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">styles.css — global <code>:focus-visible</code></div>
            <div class="option-note">
              No focus class on this button: the ring can only be coming from the global rule.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="option">
            <div class="option-label">On white — the weak case, kept on purpose</div>
            <div class="stage stage-white" style="margin-top: 0;">
              <button class="group-by-type-toggle">Tab to me</button>
            </div>
            <div class="option-where">styles.css — same rule, ~1.35:1 against white</div>
            <div class="option-note">
              This is the surface to check first when something looks wrong. See the known
              limitation below.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>Known limitation: the ring is weak on white
          <span class="badge badge-drift">unresolved</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="section-note">
          Light-pink was chosen for the dark grounds that make up most of the app, and there it is
          excellent. On the app's <strong>flat-white</strong> surfaces it falls below
          <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html">WCAG 2.2
          SC 1.4.11</a>, which asks for <strong>3:1</strong> for a focus indicator. This is a
          known, recorded gap — <strong>not</strong> a licence to add a local override.
        </p>
        <table class="gallery-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Ground</th><th>Where</th><th>Ring contrast</th><th>vs 3:1</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Dark purple gradient</td><td><code>site.css</code> — <code>/</code>, <code>/choose-any-deck</code></td><td>~10.9:1</td><td>passes comfortably</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Playmat art (both play pages)</td><td><code>prepare.css</code>, <code>game.css</code></td><td>varies with the art</td><td>unmeasured — pale passages are the risk</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Flat white</strong></td><td><code>.modal-dialog</code>, <code>docs.css</code>, <code>.button-base:disabled</code></td><td><strong>~1.35:1</strong></td><td><strong>fails</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="section-note">
          <strong>Why it is still open.</strong> The sanctioned fix is a hairline
          <code>--deep-space</code> companion behind the ring — not a different color. But a halo
          can only be drawn with <code>box-shadow</code>, and <code>box-shadow</code> does not
          accumulate across rules: adding one would <em>erase</em> the two-layer press bevel that
          every button gets from <code>.pushable-flat</code> (see <a href="#buttons">Buttons</a>).
          A pseudo-element is no escape either, because an <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> cannot have
          one. So the real fix means re-declaring the bevel inside <code>:focus-visible</code> for
          both <code>.pushable-flat</code> and <code>.pushable-flat.pushable-dark</code>. That is
          a decision with a cost, so it waits for Jess rather than being patched in locally.
        </p>
        <p class="section-note">
          <strong>And it cannot be judged yet.</strong> The white surface that matters most is the
          modal interior — but the modals manage no focus at all: opening one leaves focus on the
          button behind the overlay, and Tab walks the page underneath instead of staying inside.
          Fix that first, then tab into a modal and decide whether the ring on white is really a
          problem in practice. Tracked in <code>TODO.md</code> as
          <code>modals-are-not-modal</code>, <code>focus-ring-manual-tabthrough</code> and
          <code>focus-ring-on-white-decision</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ================================================================ -->
      <section class="gallery-section" id="inputs">
        <h2>Text inputs <span class="badge badge-choose">choose one</span></h2>
        <p class="section-note">
